lesson art-mixing-in-proportion v=1 format=teach grade=4 unit=1 subject=art levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Mixing in parts"
  goal "Mix colours in parts, scale a recipe up so the colour stays the same, and compare mixes by the share of each paint."

  look {
    scene 13x7 {
      mixingtray tray wells=["yellow 2+blue", "yellow+blue", "blue 2+yellow"] ring=-1 labels=true at=canvas(1, 1)
    }
    say """
      How much of each paint matters. Two parts yellow to one part blue makes a yellow green, like a
      new leaf. One part yellow to two parts blue makes a blue green, like a pine tree. Mixing in
      parts means you can make the same green again tomorrow.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice art.two-to-one count=2 seed=2
    level easy {
      worked art.same-green a=3 b=1 m=3 p=0 level=medium
    }
    practice art.same-green count=3 seed=4
    practice art.scale-the-mix count=2 hard-count=1 seed=3
    level hard {
      practice art.more-yellow count=1 seed=1
    }
    show art.three-greens
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show art.more-yellow k=0 p=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show art.how-many-greens n=3 easy-n=2 hard-n=4
  }
  remember {
    say "The same green needs the same parts of each paint for every part of the other. Double one, double the other."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Mixing in parts is ratio, met a year or two before it is taught by name. The first questions
    name what two parts to one make. The next ones scale a recipe up: twice as much of the same
    green needs twice as much of each paint, and the option that adds one more of each is there
    because nearly every child picks it first. The machine proves each answer with the same mixing
    the brush uses, so the green it calls the same really is identical and the one it calls
    different really is a different green, however close it looks.

    The leaf painting in the middle of the lesson is the child's, marked by a grown-up. The two-star
    question compares two greens where the one with more yellow in it is not the yellower one, so
    the child has to compare yellow for each part of blue. The three-star question asks how many
    different greens up to three parts of each can make. It has no method on the page: children
    list pairs, notice that two and two is the same green as one and one, and cross out the
    repeats. A child who gets stuck there has not failed the lesson.

    Respond to the leaf by naming the greens: "This one is yellower, like a new leaf, and this one
    has more blue." Ask which green they would make more of, and how they would keep it the same.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the pots are as written, three times a green is mixed once in the teacher's pen before greens doubled, the ringed greens are doubled, the three greens are as written, the two-star is as written, and the three-star counts greens from one or two parts."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the pots are as written, the greens are made three times over, the ringed greens are tripled or quadrupled and one of them makes way for a second pair of greens to compare, the three greens are as written, the two-star is as written, and the three-star counts greens from one to four parts."
  }
}
